{
  "concept": "At the top of every iteration of PARTITION's loop the subarray is split into four regions — A[p..i] ≤ x, A[i+1..j-1] > x, A[j..r-1] unexamined, and the pivot x at A[r] — and each iteration restores this invariant by either advancing j alone or swapping and advancing both i and j.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Long horizontal strip of equal-width rectangular cells representing the subarray A[p..r]",
    "Italic index labels above the strip: p over the leftmost cell, i over the last pale-gray cell, j over the first unexamined cell, r over the rightmost cell",
    "Three heavy black vertical bars drawn between cells marking the boundaries at i, at j-1/j, and just before r",
    "Pale-gray shaded run of cells from p through i (the ≤ x region)",
    "Darker-gray shaded run of cells from i+1 through j-1 (the > x region)",
    "White unshaded run of cells from j through r-1 (the unrestricted / unexamined region)",
    "Single white cell at the far right under r containing the italic letter x (the pivot)",
    "Three curly braces beneath the strip labelling the regions: '≤ x', '> x', 'unrestricted'",
    "Numeric value drawn inside each cell (element values of the working array)",
    "Highlight ring / arrow on the cell currently at index j being compared",
    "Swap indicator: curved double-headed arrow between cells i and j when a swap occurs",
    "Comparison readout annotation, e.g. 'A[j] = 1 ≤ x = 4'",
    "Frame counter / step caption line showing the note text for the current recorded step",
    "Legend mapping pale gray = ≤ x, dark gray = > x, white = unrestricted, boxed x = pivot"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "partitionCode",
      "type": "code_editor",
      "teaches": "That the four-region picture is not decoration but a loop invariant the code must actively maintain: the learner writes the loop and watches the boundaries i and j move exactly as their own comparisons and swaps dictate, and sees the regions break when the logic is wrong.",
      "label": "Implement PARTITION(A, p, r)",
      "entry_point": "partition(A, p, r)",
      "api": [
        { "op": "pivot()", "does": "returns the pivot value x = A[r] (read only)" },
        { "op": "value(k)", "does": "returns A[k] without changing anything" },
        { "op": "lessEq(k)", "does": "true when A[k] <= pivot(); records the comparison in the frame" },
        { "op": "swap(a, b)", "does": "exchange A[a] and A[b] — the ONLY way to mutate the array" },
        { "op": "setI(k)", "does": "move the i boundary bar to k, redrawing the ≤ x region as A[p..k]" },
        { "op": "setJ(k)", "does": "move the j scan bar to k, redrawing the > x and unrestricted regions" },
        { "op": "note(text)", "does": "attach your own caption to the current frame" }
      ],
      "frame": "the whole strip with current shading of the four regions, the positions of the i and j bars, the value of x, and the one or two cells touched by the last op (compared cell ringed, swapped pair joined by an arc)",
      "input": { "label": "Initial subarray A[p..r] (pivot is the last value)", "default": "2, 8, 7, 1, 3, 5, 6, 4" },
      "sample_code": "function partition(A, p, r) {\n  const x = pivot();            // x = A[r]\n  let i = p - 1;\n  setI(i);                      // \u2264 x region is empty\n  for (let j = p; j < r; j++) {\n    setJ(j);\n    if (lessEq(j)) {            // A[j] \u2264 x\n      i = i + 1;\n      setI(i);\n      swap(i, j);               // move it into the \u2264 x region\n      note('A[j] \u2264 x: grow \u2264 x region, both bars advance');\n    } else {\n      note('A[j] > x: only j advances, > x region grows');\n    }\n  }\n  swap(i + 1, r);               // put the pivot between the regions\n  setI(i + 1);\n  return i + 1;\n}",
      "buggy_code": "function partition(A, p, r) {\n  const x = pivot();\n  let i = p - 1;\n  setI(i);\n  for (let j = p; j < r; j++) {\n    setJ(j);\n    if (lessEq(j)) {\n      swap(i, j);               // BUG: swaps before incrementing i,\n      i = i + 1;                // so the element lands one cell too far left\n      setI(i);                  // and a > x value leaks into the \u2264 x region\n    }\n  }\n  swap(i + 1, r);\n  setI(i + 1);\n  return i + 1;\n}",
      "success_check": "the returned index q satisfies: every cell in A[p..q-1] is \u2264 A[q], A[q] equals the original pivot x, and every cell in A[q+1..r] is > A[q]"
    },
    {
      "id": "caseFocus",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Why the two branches differ: when A[j] \u2264 x the \u2264 x region must grow, which requires a swap to evict the > x element sitting at i+1; when A[j] > x the > x region simply absorbs the cell and i never moves.",
      "label": "Jump to next iteration where",
      "options": ["A[j] \u2264 x (swap branch)", "A[j] > x (skip branch)"],
      "default": "A[j] > x (skip branch)"
    },
    {
      "id": "invariantOverlay",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That each region's defining property can be checked cell by cell — the shading is a claim about values, and a violated cell is what a buggy loop produces.",
      "label": "Verify invariant on every cell",
      "default": true
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Read the picture before running anything",
      "narration": "The strip shows the state at the top of one iteration: pale gray cells are already known \u2264 x, dark gray cells are known > x, white cells have not been looked at, and the pivot x sits alone at position r. The bars at i and j are the only things that move.",
      "state": { "invariantOverlay": true, "caseFocus": "A[j] > x (skip branch)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The skip branch: A[j] > x",
      "narration": "When the scanned cell is greater than the pivot, nothing is exchanged — j advances by one and the dark gray > x region simply swallows the cell. The i bar and the pale gray region are untouched.",
      "state": { "caseFocus": "A[j] > x (skip branch)", "invariantOverlay": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "The swap branch: A[j] \u2264 x",
      "narration": "When the scanned cell is at most the pivot, the \u2264 x region must grow by one cell, but that cell is currently occupied by a > x value. Incrementing i and swapping A[i] with A[j] moves the small value left and pushes the big value to the far end of the > x region — both bars advance.",
      "state": { "caseFocus": "A[j] \u2264 x (swap branch)", "invariantOverlay": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Write the loop yourself",
      "narration": "Implement partition with lessEq, swap, setI and setJ, then replay it frame by frame. Every boundary you see move is one of your own setI/setJ calls; every recolored cell is one of your swaps.",
      "state": { "invariantOverlay": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Break it on purpose",
      "narration": "Load the buggy version that swaps before incrementing i. With invariant checking on, watch a cell greater than x appear inside the pale gray region — the picture stops being true, and the final array is no longer partitioned.",
      "state": { "invariantOverlay": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Termination: place the pivot",
      "narration": "When j reaches r the unrestricted region is empty, so the array is just \u2264 x followed by > x followed by the pivot. One final swap of A[i+1] with A[r] drops the pivot exactly on the seam, and i+1 is the returned split point.",
      "state": { "invariantOverlay": true }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The i bar is the frontier of what is known small and the j bar is the frontier of what has been looked at at all; every iteration examines exactly one new cell and the only question is whether that cell forces the small-region frontier to move too — which is precisely what the single swap accomplishes.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Draw one wide horizontal strip of ~16 equal cells with thin black outlines, centered on a light background. Italic serif labels p, i, j, r sit above the strip aligned to the first cell, the last pale-gray cell, the first white cell, and the last cell respectively. Three thick black vertical bars (much heavier than the cell borders) sit on the cell boundaries at i, before j, and before r. Color runs: pale gray (#e8e8e8) from p to i, medium gray (#a8a8a8) from i+1 to j-1, white from j to r-1, and a white pivot cell under r containing an italic x. Beneath the strip, three thin curly braces span the three runs with centered captions '\u2264 x', '> x', and 'unrestricted' in serif type. During replay, show the array's numeric values inside cells, ring the cell at j in a strong accent color (orange) when compared, and draw a curved double-headed arc above the strip between cells i and j during a swap. Keep the code editor panel to the left or below with the replay scrubber and a caption line showing the current note(). Invariant-violation cells should get a red hatched fill so a wrong implementation is visually obvious against the intended gray shading."
}

